  {h:'8:00 AM',ev:'She wakes up.'},
  {h:'9:00 AM',ev:'She takes the bus.'},
  {h:'9:30 AM',ev:'She starts working.'},
  {h:'2:00 PM',ev:'She has lunch in the cafeteria.'},
  {h:'2:00 PM',ev:'She is in a meeting.'},
  {h:'3:30 PM',ev:'She finishes working.'},
  {h:'4:00 PM',ev:'Her classes start.'},
  {h:'8:00 PM',ev:'Her classes finish.'},
  {h:'8:00 PM',ev:'She goes to the gym.'}],
 recusa:'That one is on its own. Look for a time that appears twice.',
 quase:'Eight is tight, but it is possible. Look for the one that is not.',
 done:'She is in a meeting and eating lunch at the same minute.',
 teacher:{sec:'7 min',
  do:'<b>Do NOT point.</b> Let him scan the column of times &mdash; the answer is a repeated number, and finding a repeated number is something he can do without a word of English. Then make him say WHY, in English, out loud.',
  watch:'Some will flag the eight PM pair too, and they are half right: finishing class and going to the gym at eight is tight but possible. Finishing work and sitting down to lunch at the same minute is not. That difference is worth thirty seconds of argument.',
  stuck:'Ask him to read the times out loud, top to bottom, and stop when a number repeats.',
  strong:'Ask what time each of the two things would really have to happen.'}},

/* ── VIII · INVESTIGATION · a conta que não fecha ────────────── */
{type:'hrsoma',ch:'VIII · Add It Up',min:6,layout:'wide',modo:'INVESTIGATION',
 t:'How long is her day?',
 pre:'Start time, end time, and how long it lasts. A calculator would do this in a second &mdash; reading it out loud is the part that is language.',
 como:'Read the block out loud. <b>Then</b> take the hours.',
 blocos:[
  {de:'8:00 AM',ate:'9:30 AM',o:'morning routine and the bus',hs:1.5},
  {de:'9:30 AM',ate:'3:30 PM',o:'work',hs:6},
  {de:'2:00 PM',ate:'3:00 PM',o:'lunch in the cafeteria',hs:1},
  {de:'3:30 PM',ate:'4:00 PM',o:'the bicycle',hs:0.5},
  {de:'4:00 PM',ate:'8:00 PM',o:'classes',hs:4},
  {de:'8:00 PM',ate:'10:00 PM',o:'gym and friends',hs:2},
  {de:'10:00 PM',ate:'12:00 AM',o:'reading in bed',hs:2}],
 dorme:{o:'sleeping',hs:8},
 done:'Twenty-five hours. A day has twenty-four, and hers has one more.',
 teacher:{sec:'6 min',
  do:'Every block goes through his mouth before the number appears. What the scene trains is reading a START and an END and getting a LENGTH out of them &mdash; and that is language, not arithmetic.',
  watch:'Do not let him jump to the total. The total is the punchline; the seven readings are the joke. The extra hour is the lunch that lives inside the working day &mdash; the same one he found in the scene before.',
  stuck:'Do the first block with him out loud: from eight to half past nine is an hour and a half.',
  strong:'Ask him to find the hour she lived twice.'}},

/* ── IX · IMMERSIVE · não é um dia ───────────────────────────── */
{type:'hrverdade',ch:'IX · It Isn&rsquo;t One Day',min:5,layout:'imgcon',modo:'IMMERSIVE',
